# Even content spacing

When a layout puts space around content, the spaces that belong together are equal. This applies
on web, management-web, and mobile.

A row's padding above an image matches the padding below it. A column of siblings uses one gap.
The inset on the left of a block matches the inset on the right when those edges are the same
kind of margin.

## Do

- Use one token, or one shared helper, for both sides of a seam that should match: top and bottom
  padding, left and right inset, or the gap between every sibling in a stack.
- Prefer a column or row `gap` so a missing sibling does not leave a stray margin behind.
- Keep a flush edge flush. A hairline, a full-bleed image, or a control that sits on the screen
  edge stays there. Do not add padding so the other side has a partner.

## Don't

- Don't add a few points on one side of a padding, margin, or gap to correct font metrics, line
  boxes, or icon weight. The measured gap stays even.
- Don't mix two spacing tokens on opposite sides of the same seam (`base` above and `md` below).
- Don't invent padding, margin, or gap where the layout has none.

Aligning a label's ink with a neighboring control (a switch, a glyph) is alignment. It is not a
reason to pad one side of the row more than the other.

